<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. A Letter to a Priest</h4>
<p>Choose a priest who has had an impact on your life (your pastor, a confessor, a chaplain). Write a short letter thanking him for three specific things. If he is alive, would you send it to him this very week? If he has passed away, write it as a prayer.</p>

<h4>B. The Three Degrees in Drawing</h4>
<p>Draw three simple symbols representing the bishop (crozier and miter), the priest (stole and chalice), and the deacon (dalmatic and Book of the Gospels). Next to each, write his primary function. If you can't draw, you may cut out images from the internet or simply write the names.</p>

<h4>C. Biblical Search: The Laying on of Hands</h4>
<p>Search the New Testament for at least three passages where the laying on of hands is mentioned to confer a ministry (hint: Acts 6:1-6; 1 Tm 4:14; 2 Tm 1:6). Read them and note: what do they have in common? Why does the Church continue to use this same gesture at ordinations?</p>

<h4>D. Prayer for Vocations</h4>
<p>Commit to praying each day this week for the seminarians of your diocese and for the young people God is calling to the priesthood. Holy Thursday is traditionally a special day of prayer for priests. If you don't know how many seminarians are in your diocese, look it up online. You might also want to look up the name of your diocesan vocations director and include him in your prayers.</p>

<h4>E. Reflection: The Hands of a Priest</h4>
<p>A priest's hands are consecrated to touch the Body of Christ. Think about what those hands do each day: they bless, they absolve, they anoint the sick, they lift up the Eucharist. Write a short prayer specifically for the hands of a priest you know &mdash; that they may always be instruments of grace and never of harm.</p>

</section>

